WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.019 --> 00:00:02.686
(upbeat chimes)

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:08.610
- The most important thing to know,

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:11.560
if you only know one thing,
is that disaster robots

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:14.483
make the disaster go away faster.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:19.120
My name is Robin Murphy.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.420
I'm a Professor of Computer
Science and Engineering

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:25.243
at Texas A&amp;M, and I work
with disaster robots.

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:30.960
1995, the Oklahoma City bombing happened.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.250
One of my grad students
went up and helped.

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:36.407
And he came back and said,
"We've gotta do it differently.

00:00:36.407 --> 00:00:38.757
"We could have had robots
like are being designed

00:00:38.757 --> 00:00:41.927
"to go to Mars to get
into places where people

00:00:41.927 --> 00:00:43.287
"and dogs can't go."

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:48.280
When we're talking about
our robots either ground,

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:51.770
aerial, or marine robots,
that can help responders

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:54.043
do things that they
can't do any other way.

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:58.040
And so in 1995,

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:00.883
we all started working
toward disaster robots.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:07.276
It was 2001 before we actually used one.

00:01:07.276 --> 00:01:10.190
(fire burning and crackling)

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:13.963
That was the first use of
ground robots for a disaster.

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:19.630
It's the ability to do things
you couldn't do any other way.

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:23.123
They needed to get more than
18 feet into the rubble.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:27.000
Robots can go into these places to get

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.400
to where there might have been survivors.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:33.310
If I can see what I need to
see, I can make good decisions

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:36.233
to keep the responders safe.

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:40.913
So this is the part where we
make fun of me for driving.

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:46.590
So notice that right now,
I'm driving heads up.

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:49.000
But really the trick of
a robot is to get out,

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.210
get beyond visual line of sight.

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:52.810
Because that's really the point.

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:59.610
Disaster City is one of the
emergency management complexes

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:02.050
that Texas A&amp;M has.

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:06.130
It's designed to test and to
train search and rescue teams

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:08.423
on how to conduct search
and rescue missions.

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:13.670
We've supplied robots for 28 disasters,

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:16.570
earthquakes, Hurricane Harvey.

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:21.013
We assisted with the Fukushima
Daiichi nuclear accident.

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:27.470
When we go, we bring robots
and people that we've tested

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:30.173
and practice with in
our training exercises.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:34.190
One of the biggest
challenges to doing work

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:37.513
and rescue robotics is not the robotics.

00:02:37.513 --> 00:02:39.563
It's the everything else.

00:02:40.737 --> 00:02:43.237
(soft chimes)

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:46.660
You're going to a different world.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:49.430
It's really challenging
to be at a disaster.

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:54.430
There's a physiological and
psychological impact of that.

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:56.980
It really takes quite a toll.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:02:59.530
So you have to be really
good at what you're doing.

00:03:02.733 --> 00:03:06.500
But my job is so incredibly fulfilling.

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:08.640
It's about the science and the technology,

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:12.000
and the way it could be
used for societal good.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:13.200
That's a big deal to me.

00:03:19.368 --> 00:03:22.201
(electronic beep)

